1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:08,626
A photograph of a stream can raise an important question. But a single
image rarely gives someone enough evidence to answer it.

2
00:00:08,626 --> 00:00:16,014
Streamproof is a field notebook for a better second look. It preserves
the source, checks whether a photograph supports comparison,

3
00:00:16,014 --> 00:00:23,401
and asks for the next useful observation. What you are seeing is the
actual local prototype, beginning with a historical,

4
00:00:23,401 --> 00:00:24,509
openly licensed photograph.

5
00:00:26,009 --> 00:00:33,601
The water region is selected explicitly. OpenCV checks image detail,
exposure, bright reflections and the size of that region.

6
00:00:33,601 --> 00:00:40,393
This photograph passes all four engineering checks, so it becomes a
baseline. The next request is practical:

7
00:00:40,393 --> 00:00:48,383
return to the same viewpoint in similar light, with stable bank
landmarks still in the frame. These are image measurements.

8
00:00:48,383 --> 00:00:51,180
They are not measurements of water chemistry.

9
00:00:52,680 --> 00:01:00,817
Now we use a clearly labeled programmed control. One patch has been
deliberately recolored, while the bank stays fixed.

10
00:01:00,817 --> 00:01:08,526
OpenCV aligns features outside the water, checks the background for a
lighting shift, and measures the overlapping region.

11
00:01:08,526 --> 00:01:15,807
About sixty-seven percent of its pixels exceed the display-distance
threshold. The policy asks for a close view,

12
00:01:15,807 --> 00:01:23,516
an upstream view and context. The overlay and decision trace show
exactly why. No pollution diagnosis is generated.

13
00:01:25,016 --> 00:01:34,563
A different input needs a different response. Here the entire scene
becomes brighter, and we compare it with the original control baseline.

14
00:01:34,563 --> 00:01:43,677
The apparent change is large, but the background changes too.
Streamproof marks the comparison inconclusive and requests similar light
and exposure.

15
00:01:43,677 --> 00:01:50,186
That distinction matters: a dramatic pixel difference should not
automatically become a dramatic environmental claim.

16
00:01:51,686 --> 00:01:58,753
The blur control fails even earlier. Image detail falls below the
quality threshold, so comparison stops.

17
00:01:58,753 --> 00:02:05,377
Instead of inventing an explanation, the notebook requests a steadier
photograph with textured bank features.

18
00:02:05,377 --> 00:02:14,210
The failed observation is still preserved. This makes the limits of the
evidence visible, rather than quietly discarding inconvenient inputs.

19
00:02:15,710 --> 00:02:22,009
Interpretation is a separate, recorded step. This example is explicitly
signed as an AI-assisted demo operator,

20
00:02:22,009 --> 00:02:31,065
and the note says the patch was deliberately recolored. It is not a
field assessment. In actual use, a person would record what

21
00:02:31,065 --> 00:02:40,121
they know, what remains uncertain, and what evidence is still needed.
The application stores that assessment alongside the original
measurements without treating a

22
00:02:40,121 --> 00:02:42,877
completed form as proof of water safety.

23
00:02:44,377 --> 00:02:51,472
Export the source fingerprints, selected regions, measurements,
decisions and review as an inspectable evidence packet.

24
00:02:51,472 --> 00:03:00,931
A separate verifier checks its linked audit records. That establishes
internal consistency, not where or when a photograph was taken.

25
00:03:00,931 --> 00:03:09,918
The prototype runs real OpenCV five with twenty-four passing local
tests. Field calibration and expert evaluation are still ahead.

26
00:03:09,918 --> 00:03:15,120
Streamproof: notice a change, keep the evidence, and let people decide.
